<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Guides', href: '/docs/guides' }, { label: 'Running Your First Ticket' }]}>
  <h1>Running Your First Ticket</h1>

  <p>
    This guide walks a single issue from creation to merged pull request, so you know exactly
    what to expect at every step — and what to do if something needs your attention.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you begin</h2>
  <p>
    You've completed the Quickstart: a team is running for your repository, GitHub OAuth is
    linked, and (ideally) notifications are on.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 1 — Open a clearly-scoped issue</h2>
  <p>
    Create a GitHub issue in the bound repository. The more complete the description, the
    better the plan the builder produces. Include the goal, any constraints, and acceptance
    criteria if you have them.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 2 — The orchestrator picks it up</h2>
  <p>
    Within a cycle, the orchestrator (NEXUS) ingests your issue as a <strong>ticket</strong>.
    You'll see it appear in the control panel's status view under open tickets.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 3 — The builder plans, then halts</h2>
  <p>
    The builder (FORGE) reads the ticket and writes a <strong>plan</strong> for how it will
    implement it — then <strong>stops</strong>. It does not start coding. The ticket enters the
    planning state.
  </p>
  <p><em>If it stays here:</em> that's expected. The builder is waiting for plan approval.</p>

  <h2>Step 4 — The reviewer examines the plan</h2>
  <p>
    The reviewer (SENTINEL) examines the plan. On approval, the planning gate opens; on issues,
    the builder revises and resubmits.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>If you're notified here:</em> the plan may have raised an ambiguity or a concern that
    needs your judgment. Answer it (see <a href="/docs/guides/handling-human-escalations">Handling Human Escalations</a>) and work resumes.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 5 — The builder builds and opens a PR</h2>
  <p>
    With the plan approved, the builder implements it, runs tests, and opens a pull request.
    The ticket moves through building, then testing, then review ready.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 6 — The reviewer reviews the code adversarially</h2>
  <p>
    The reviewer attacks the diff for security, quality, and test coverage — from its own
    isolated workspace. It can request a delegated test run as evidence before deciding.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Approve</strong> — the ticket proceeds to merge.</li>
    <li><strong>Reject</strong> — the builder revises based on the review feedback, and the cycle repeats.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step 7 — The mergers merge</h2>
  <p>
    The mergers (VESSEL) watch CI, resolve any conflicts, and merge the approved pull request.
    The ticket becomes merged and the workspace is torn down.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 8 — (Optional) The writer documents</h2>
  <p>
    If the writer role is enabled, it updates changelogs and project history for the merged
    change.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 9 — You get the result</h2>
  <p>
    You have a <strong>reviewed, production-ready pull request</strong>. You can inspect it on
    GitHub, and the control panel shows the full record of decisions made along the way.
  </p>

  <h2>What can interrupt this path</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A human decision</strong> — security, ambiguity, architecture, or a merge block. You're notified; resolve it and the team continues.</li>
    <li><strong>An automatic recovery</strong> — a crashed workspace or silent worker is detected and re-provisioned. You generally don't need to do anything.</li>
    <li><strong>A conflict or CI failure</strong> — the mergers handle or reassign these automatically; only an unresolvable case escalates.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What this feels like in practice</h2>
  <p>
    For a well-scoped issue, the whole path runs end-to-end with no input from you. You open an
    issue, and later you have a merged PR waiting on GitHub, with the panel showing the journey.
    Your involvement is reserved for the decisions that genuinely need a person.
  </p>

  <Callout type="tip" title="Plan before code">
    The builder always plans and halts before it codes, and the reviewer must approve the plan
    before the gate opens. That's the "plan before code" guardrail working — it keeps a
    well-considered approach ahead of implementation.
  </Callout>
</DocsLayout>
